================================================================
SECTION 1 — WHAT THIS PROJECT BUILDS
================================================================

This project builds a complete AWS network from scratch using
Terraform. You run two commands and in 5 minutes you have a
fully working production-grade network.

Resources created:
  - 1 VPC (10.0.0.0/16)
  - 1 Internet Gateway
  - 2 Public Subnets (AZ-a and AZ-b)
  - 2 Private Subnets (AZ-a and AZ-b)
  - 2 Elastic IPs
  - 2 NAT Gateways (one per AZ)
  - 1 Public Route Table
  - 2 Private Route Tables (one per AZ)
  - 3 Security Groups (Bastion, Private Instance, ALB)
  - 1 Bastion Host EC2
  - 1 S3 Bucket (remote state)
  - 1 DynamoDB Table (state locking)


================================================================
SECTION 2 — WHAT EACH COMPONENT DOES AND WHY
================================================================

VPC (10.0.0.0/16)
  What  : Your private isolated network inside AWS
  Where : Container for everything — all resources live here
  Why   : Without VPC nothing is isolated. Everything would be
          on shared public AWS infrastructure

Internet Gateway (IGW)
  What  : The door between your VPC and the internet
  Where : Attached to the VPC
  Why   : Without IGW no traffic enters or leaves the VPC.
          Even instances with public IPs cannot communicate.
          AWS limit: 1 IGW per VPC

Public Subnets (10.0.1.0/24 in AZ-a, 10.0.2.0/24 in AZ-b)
  What  : Subnets where instances get public IPs automatically
  Where : map_public_ip_on_launch = true
  Why   : Bastion needs public IP to be SSH-able from internet.
          NAT Gateway must be in public subnet.
          Load Balancer must face the internet.
  Note  : Bastion is only in ONE public subnet (AZ-a).
          Second public subnet exists for NAT GW and future ALB.
          ALB requires minimum 2 subnets in different AZs.

Private Subnets (10.0.10.0/24 in AZ-a, 10.0.20.0/24 in AZ-b)
  What  : Subnets where instances NEVER get public IPs
  Where : map_public_ip_on_launch = false
  Why   : App servers and databases must never be directly
          reachable from the internet. Only reachable via
          Bastion (SSH) or Load Balancer (HTTP).

Public Route Table
  What  : Rule that says send internet traffic through IGW
  Where : Shared by BOTH public subnets
  Why   : Without this rule even though IGW exists, traffic
          has no path to use it. Route table is the map.
  Rule  : 0.0.0.0/0 → Internet Gateway
  Note  : There is also an invisible local route:
          10.0.0.0/16 → local (stays inside VPC)

Private Route Tables (one per AZ)
  What  : Rule that sends internet traffic through NAT Gateway
  Where : One separate route table per private subnet
  Why separate per AZ? :
          If you had ONE shared private route table pointing to
          AZ-a NAT GW, and AZ-a fails → ALL private subnets in
          ALL AZs lose internet.
          With separate route tables:
            RT-1 → NAT GW in AZ-a (for AZ-a private subnet)
            RT-2 → NAT GW in AZ-b (for AZ-b private subnet)
          If AZ-a fails → only AZ-a affected, AZ-b keeps working
  Rule  : 0.0.0.0/0 → NAT Gateway (same AZ)

Elastic IPs
  What  : Static public IP addresses for NAT Gateways
  Where : One per NAT Gateway
  Why   : Private instance traffic appears on internet FROM
          these IPs. Static so they never change on restart.

NAT Gateways (one per AZ, in PUBLIC subnets)
  What  : Lets private instances reach internet without
          having a public IP
  Where : MUST be in PUBLIC subnet (common mistake: putting
          in private subnet breaks everything)
  Why   : Private EC2 needs to run apt install, docker pull,
          call external APIs. NAT GW forwards that traffic out.
  Flow  : Private EC2 → NAT GW → IGW → Internet
          Internet reply → IGW → NAT GW → Private EC2
          Internet CANNOT initiate connection to Private EC2

Bastion Host
  What  : A t2.micro EC2 — the only SSH entry point
  Where : PUBLIC subnet AZ-a only (one bastion is enough)
  Why   : Instead of opening SSH on every private server,
          you SSH to bastion first, then from bastion you
          SSH to private servers.
          One attack surface instead of many.
  Flow  : Your laptop → SSH → Bastion → SSH → Private EC2

Security Group — Bastion SG
  Inbound  : Port 22 from YOUR IP only (set in tfvars)
  Outbound : Everything (needs to reach private instances)
  Attached : Bastion EC2

Security Group — Private Instance SG
  Inbound  : Port 22 from Bastion SG only (SG reference)
             Port 80 from ALB SG only (SG reference)
  Outbound : Everything (for apt install, docker pull)
  Attached : App server EC2s (attached when you create them)
  Note     : This SG is created here but attached to private
             EC2s when you create them in a future project

Security Group — ALB SG
  Inbound  : Port 80 from internet (0.0.0.0/0)
             Port 443 from internet (0.0.0.0/0)
  Outbound : Everything (to forward to private instances)
  Attached : Load Balancer (created in a future project)
  Why created now : Private SG already has a rule trusting
                    ALB SG. When you add ALB later it just works.

S3 Bucket
  What  : Stores terraform.tfstate remotely
  Why   : Without it state is on your laptop — team cannot
          collaborate and you risk losing it.
  Extra : Versioning enabled (recover old state if corrupted)
          Encryption enabled (state contains sensitive data)
          Public access blocked (must never be public)

DynamoDB Table
  What  : State locking
  Why   : If two engineers run terraform apply at same time,
          both read same state, both make changes → corruption.
          DynamoDB: first apply writes a lock, second apply
          sees the lock and waits. No corruption possible.


================================================================
SECTION 3 — SG-TO-SG REFERENCING EXPLAINED
================================================================

Normal way (by IP/CIDR):
  allow port 22 from 10.0.1.0/24

Problem: This allows ANYONE in that subnet range to SSH.
         If subnet changes, you must update the rule.

Better way (SG reference):
  allow port 22 from <Bastion SG ID>

This means: only instances that have Bastion SG attached
            can SSH here. Not an IP range. Exact machine.

Example:
  Hacker gets a machine inside VPC
  Their machine = 10.0.1.50
  CIDR rule = allows 10.0.1.0/24 → hacker gets in (BAD)
  SG rule = only bastion SG → hacker blocked (GOOD)

The three SG rules in this project:
  Bastion SG   → uses cidr (your IP) for inbound SSH
  Private SG   → uses SG reference for both inbound rules
  ALB SG       → uses cidr (0.0.0.0/0) for inbound web traffic

How SG attaches to EC2:
  resource "aws_instance" "bastion" {
    vpc_security_group_ids = [var.bastion_sg_id]  ← here
  }
  Private instances get private_sg attached the same way
  when you create them in a future project.


================================================================
SECTION 4 — ROUTE TABLE FLOW EXPLAINED SIMPLY
================================================================

People confuse this. Route tables control OUTBOUND traffic
from a subnet, not inbound.

Public subnet traffic flow:
  EC2 in public subnet wants to reach google.com
  → Checks public route table
  → Rule: 0.0.0.0/0 → IGW
  → Traffic goes through IGW to internet
  → Reply comes back through IGW

Private subnet traffic flow:
  EC2 in private subnet wants to run apt install
  → Checks private route table
  → Rule: 0.0.0.0/0 → NAT GW (same AZ)
  → Traffic goes to NAT GW (in public subnet)
  → NAT GW checks public route table
  → Rule: 0.0.0.0/0 → IGW
  → Traffic goes through IGW to internet
  → Reply comes back: IGW → NAT GW → Private EC2

Both ultimately use IGW. But private goes through NAT GW
first so private instances stay hidden from internet.


================================================================
SECTION 5 — count AND count.index EXPLAINED
================================================================

You have two lists:
  public_subnet_cidrs = ["10.0.1.0/24", "10.0.2.0/24"]
  availability_zones  = ["us-east-1a",  "us-east-1b" ]

Think of them as paired columns:
  Index   CIDR           AZ
    0     10.0.1.0/24    us-east-1a
    1     10.0.2.0/24    us-east-1b

count = length(var.public_subnet_cidrs) = 2
So Terraform loops twice: count.index = 0, then 1

First loop (count.index = 0):
  cidr_block        = public_subnet_cidrs[0] = 10.0.1.0/24
  availability_zone = availability_zones[0]  = us-east-1a
  Creates subnet 1 in AZ-a

Second loop (count.index = 1):
  cidr_block        = public_subnet_cidrs[1] = 10.0.2.0/24
  availability_zone = availability_zones[1]  = us-east-1b
  Creates subnet 2 in AZ-b

Both lines use SAME count.index so CIDR and AZ stay paired.
If you used different indexes they would get mixed up.


================================================================
SECTION 6 — enable_dns_hostnames AND enable_dns_support
================================================================

enable_dns_support = true
  Enables DNS resolver inside VPC at address 10.0.0.2
  Without this: nothing inside VPC can resolve any domain
  apt install fails because archive.ubuntu.com cannot resolve
  docker pull fails because registry cannot resolve
  This is the DNS resolver itself — must be true for anything
  DNS related to work

enable_dns_hostnames = true
  EC2 instances get a public DNS name like:
  ec2-54-123-45-67.compute-1.amazonaws.com
  Without this: EC2 has IP but no hostname
  RDS endpoints like mydb.abc.rds.amazonaws.com wont work
  EKS nodes cannot register with control plane

Rule: both must be true together. One without other = broken.


================================================================
SECTION 7 — WHY NAT GATEWAY IN PUBLIC SUBNET NOT PRIVATE
================================================================

Most common mistake people make.

NAT Gateway needs:
  1. To receive traffic from private instances
  2. To forward that traffic OUT to the internet
  3. To receive replies from internet
  4. To send replies back to private instances

Step 2 and 3 require internet access.
To have internet access it must be in a public subnet
(which has a route to IGW).

If you put NAT GW in private subnet:
  Private subnet has no route to IGW
  NAT GW cannot reach internet
  Private instances lose all outbound internet access

Always: NAT GW goes in PUBLIC subnet.


================================================================
SECTION 8 — WHY ALB SG EXISTS WITHOUT AN ALB
================================================================

The private instance SG has this rule:
  allow port 80 from ALB SG

This rule references ALB SG by its ID.
So ALB SG must exist for the rule to reference it.
The actual Load Balancer does not need to exist yet.

When you create the ALB in a future project:
  - You attach ALB SG to it
  - Private instances already trust that SG
  - Everything works without changing any security group rules

This is forward planning. Build the network foundation now.
Application layer (ALB, EC2, RDS) comes on top later.

Why no ALB in this project:
  - ALB costs ~$16/month even when idle
  - VPC and SGs cost nothing on their own
  - Keeps this project focused on network layer only
  - Other projects reuse this VPC as their foundation


================================================================
SECTION 9 — HOW MODULES CONNECT IN main.tf
================================================================

main.tf does not create any AWS resources directly.
It calls modules and connects their outputs to other modules.

Data flow:

  terraform.tfvars
      |
      ├─ vpc_cidr ──────────────→ module.vpc
      |                               |
      |                          vpc_id, igw_id
      |                               |
      ├─ subnet cidrs ──────────→ module.subnets ←── nat_gateway_ids
      ├─ AZ list ───────────────→ module.subnets          |
      |                               |                    |
      |                        public_subnet_ids           |
      |                               |                    |
      |                               └──→ module.nat_gateway
      |
      ├─ my_ip ─────────────────→ module.security_groups
      |                               |
      |                          bastion_sg_id
      |                               |
      ├─ bastion_ami ───────────→ module.bastion ←── public_subnet_ids[0]
      └─ key_pair_name ─────────→ module.bastion

Key connections:
  module.vpc.vpc_id              → feeds into subnets, security_groups
  module.vpc.igw_id              → feeds into subnets (for public route table)
  module.subnets.public_subnet_ids → feeds into nat_gateway, bastion[0]
  module.nat_gateway.nat_gateway_ids → feeds into subnets (private routes)
  module.security_groups.bastion_sg_id → feeds into bastion

Why bastion is last:
  It needs public_subnet_ids from subnets module
  It needs bastion_sg_id from security_groups module
  Both must exist first → bastion is created last

public_subnet_ids[0] means:
  Only first public subnet (AZ-a) is passed to bastion
  Bastion only goes in ONE AZ — no need for two bastions
  Second public subnet exists for NAT GW and future ALB


