SECTION 11 — FILE CREATION ORDER AND WHY
================================================================

1.  backend-setup/main.tf  → run first, creates S3 + DynamoDB
2.  backend.tf             → configure S3 as backend (after bucket exists)
3.  provider.tf            → AWS provider + default tags
4.  variables.tf           → declare all variables
5.  terraform.tfvars       → assign real values to variables
6.  modules/vpc/variables.tf      → what VPC module needs as input
7.  modules/vpc/main.tf           → VPC + IGW resources
8.  modules/vpc/outputs.tf        → expose vpc_id and igw_id
9.  modules/nat_gateway/variables.tf → inputs: public_subnet_ids
10. modules/nat_gateway/main.tf      → EIPs + NAT Gateways
11. modules/nat_gateway/outputs.tf   → expose nat_gateway_ids
12. modules/subnets/variables.tf  → inputs: vpc_id, igw_id, nat_gw_ids
13. modules/subnets/main.tf       → 4 subnets + route tables
14. modules/subnets/outputs.tf    → expose subnet ID lists
15. modules/security_groups/variables.tf → inputs: vpc_id, my_ip
16. modules/security_groups/main.tf      → 3 security groups
17. modules/security_groups/outputs.tf   → expose 3 SG IDs
18. modules/bastion/variables.tf  → inputs: subnet_id, sg_id, ami, key
19. modules/bastion/main.tf       → bastion EC2 with user_data
20. modules/bastion/outputs.tf    → expose bastion_public_ip
21. main.tf                       → wire all modules together
22. outputs.tf                    → expose final values to terminal

Inside every module always create in this order:
  1. variables.tf  (what inputs does this module need?)
  2. main.tf       (create resources using those inputs)
  3. outputs.tf    (what values does this module expose?)

Rule: variables declare → main.tf creates → outputs expose


================================================================
SECTION 12 — MODULE CREATION ORDER AND WHY
================================================================

Module 1: vpc
  Why first: No dependencies. Everything else needs vpc_id.

Module 2: nat_gateway
  Why second: Needs public_subnet_ids from subnets module.
              But subnets needs nat_gateway_ids from this module.
              Terraform resolves this — write both and wire in main.tf.
              Terraform creates public subnets first, then NAT GW,
              then private route tables. Auto-resolved.

Module 3: subnets
  Why third: Needs vpc_id (from vpc) + igw_id (from vpc)
             + nat_gateway_ids (from nat_gateway)

Module 4: security_groups
  Why fourth: Only needs vpc_id. Independent of subnets and NAT GW.
              Can be written any time after vpc module.

Module 5: bastion
  Why last: Needs public_subnet_ids[0] from subnets module
            Needs bastion_sg_id from security_groups module
            Depends on most things → created last


================================================================
SECTION 13 — COMPLETE TRAFFIC FLOWS
================================================================

User accessing your app:
  Internet
    → IGW (enters VPC)
    → ALB (in public subnet, has ALB SG — allows 80/443)
    → Private EC2 (in private subnet, has Private SG)
          Private SG allows port 80 from ALB SG only
    → App responds
    → Reply goes back: Private EC2 → ALB → IGW → Internet

You SSHing to private server:
  Your laptop (your IP)
    → IGW (enters VPC)
    → Bastion (in public subnet, has Bastion SG)
          Bastion SG allows port 22 from your IP only
    → Private EC2 (in private subnet, has Private SG)
          Private SG allows port 22 from Bastion SG only
    → You are inside private server

Private server downloading packages:
  Private EC2
    → Private Route Table (rule: 0.0.0.0/0 → NAT GW)
    → NAT GW (in public subnet)
    → Public Route Table (rule: 0.0.0.0/0 → IGW)
    → IGW
    → Internet (apt install, docker pull, etc.)
    → Reply comes back same path in reverse


