================================================================
SECTION 16 — ONE LINE ANSWERS FOR INTERVIEWS
================================================================

Q: What is a VPC?
A: Your private isolated network inside AWS

Q: What is IGW?
A: The door between VPC and internet. Without it nothing works.

Q: Why two public subnets but only one bastion?
A: Second public subnet is for NAT GW and future ALB.
   ALB requires 2 subnets in different AZs. Bastion only needs one.

Q: Why one private route table per AZ?
A: Each points to its own AZ NAT GW. If one AZ fails,
   other AZ private subnet keeps internet via its own NAT GW.

Q: Why NAT GW in public subnet not private?
A: NAT GW needs internet access to forward traffic. Only public
   subnet has a route to IGW. Private subnet has no IGW route.

Q: What is SG-to-SG referencing?
A: Instead of allowing traffic from an IP range, you allow
   traffic from instances that have a specific SG attached.
   More secure and survives IP changes.

Q: Why does ALB SG exist without an ALB?
A: Private instance SG references ALB SG ID in its rules.
   ALB SG must exist for that reference. ALB comes later.

Q: What is state locking?
A: DynamoDB prevents two terraform applies at same time.
   Prevents state corruption from concurrent runs.

Q: What is the chicken-egg problem?
A: Cannot create S3 bucket and use it as backend in same apply.
   Solution: create bucket in separate folder first.

Q: How does private SG attach to private instances?
A: Via vpc_security_group_ids argument when creating EC2.
   Done in future project when app servers are created.

================================================================

Conclusion:
This project provisions a complete, production-grade AWS network using Terraform — a VPC (10.0.0.0/16) split into 2 public and 2 private subnets across two Availability Zones, where public subnets host the Bastion Host (the single hardened SSH entry point into the network) and two NAT Gateways (one per AZ, so a failure in one zone doesn't take down internet access for the other), while private subnets are reserved for app servers and databases that are never directly reachable from the internet; traffic is routed through a shared public route table pointing to the Internet Gateway and separate private route tables per AZ each pointing to their own NAT Gateway, all secured by three Security Groups where the private instance SG uses SG-to-SG referencing instead of IP ranges (trusting only the Bastion SG for SSH and a future ALB SG for HTTP, so a security layer is pre-wired even before a Load Balancer exists), and the whole project itself is built using five small reusable Terraform modules (vpc, subnets, nat_gateway, security_groups, bastion) wired together in a root main.tf that passes outputs from one module as inputs to the next, with the entire setup's state stored remotely in an S3 bucket and locked via DynamoDB so multiple engineers can safely collaborate without corrupting it — the end result being a secure, highly available network foundation that future projects (RDS, ALB, EKS, app servers) can simply plug into rather than rebuilding from scratch.